WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.200
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.040
So after my video last week, a Nerdfighter
posted this picture to twitter with the caption,

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.180
"Who wore it better, Tom Hanks in Castaway
or John Green in Vlogbrothers?"

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.320
Also, someone in comments noted "The puff
seems to have migrated south for the winter."

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.180
So yeah, I trimmed my beard, okay.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.690
In other news, Hank, there is this line from
my book Paper Towns, "I'm in love with cities

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.760
I've never been to and people I've never met,"
and that line has been quoted a lot over the

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:23.760
years.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:24.760
I mean it pops up all the time on Tumblr.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.560
It has over seventeen hundred likes on Goodreads.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.130
Now, it's no, "If people were rain, I was
a drizzle and she was a hurricane."

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:34.829
It's not even one of the most quoted lines
from Paper Towns, but it's definitely sort

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:36.899
of widely out there in the world.

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:41.079
There is even a poster available at DFTBA.com
designed by Nerdfighter Risa.

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:45.019
Now, Hank, to be totally honest with you,
I don't remember writing the words "I'm in

00:00:45.019 --> 00:00:48.639
love with the cities I've never been to and
people I've never met" but then again, I don't

00:00:48.639 --> 00:00:50.530
remember writing a lot of Paper Towns.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:51.760
That book came out seven years ago.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.210
Sometimes I'd be on the set of the Paper Towns
movie and I would be like, "Why is this happening?"

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:58.700
and Jake Schreier, the director, would be
like, "Because it's in the book," and I'd

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.440
be like, "Oh yeah, right, how is the book
anyway?

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:02.440
Is it good?

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:03.739
I hear there is a little bit too much Walt
Whitman."

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:06.770
So yeah, when I started seeing the quote a
few years ago, I just assumed that it was

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:10.180
in Paper Towns; people kept sourcing it as
being from Paper Towns.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:13.640
I suppose instead of blindly assuming I'd
written something that the Internet said I

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.119
wrote, I should've done some research, but,
you know Hank, how many of us have failed

00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:19.310
to fact check something on the internet?

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:20.310
Most I would think.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:25.030
Flash forward to last week when I come across
a post on the copyright subreddit, and it's

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:30.060
like, "My friend wrote a thing on Tumblr that
is now being attributed to John Green; in

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.870
fact, like, they're selling a poster of it,"
and then at the end of the post she was like,

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:37.219
"You can just control-f and search for that
quote in Paper Towns; it is not there."

00:01:37.219 --> 00:01:38.219
So I did.

00:01:38.219 --> 00:01:42.140
I illegally downloaded a pirated copy of my
own novel, I hit control-f, I searched for

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:43.140
the quote.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.290
Sure enough -- not there.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:48.500
So Hank, it turns out "I'm in love with cities
I've never been and people I've never met,"

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.630
was written by a thirteen-year-old Nerdfighter
several years ago.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:57.170
On her Tumblr, she often quoted my books a
lot and made art using quotes, and so someone,

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:01.520
somewhere assumed that line was written by
me, and then yeah.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.350
Now this poor young Nerdfighter, Melody, gets
lots of hateful asks in her Tumblr inbox from

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.860
people who are like, "Why are you stealing
John Green's quote?!"

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.230
I stole it!

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.020
I am the thief!

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:17.560
So Hank, just to be clear, we've been selling
a poster with a quotation attributed to me

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.130
that was in fact written by a thirteen-year-old
Nerdfighter.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:23.370
Now obviously Melody and Risa are going to
share the royalties from that poster.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:27.560
We're retroactively paying Melody for the
posters we've sold so far, and also when I

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.140
saw this artwork of Melody's I was like, "I
really like that; we should make that a poster

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.360
at DFTBA.com," so now we have, it's available
for pre-order.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:36.170
All the royalties go to me, no, they go to
Melody.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:39.750
Now Hank, admittedly I'm a bit of a grapefruit
for getting into this situation in the first

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:40.750
place.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.270
Like, this isn't the first time that a quotation
has been misattributed to me.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:46.740
Like, there's that line from the series finale
of the television program One Tree Hill that

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.120
has been attributed to me like seven hundred
thousand times on Tumblr, but I did not write

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.120
that.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.220
But I think a wrongly-attributed author, even
a very grape-fruity one, failing to notice

00:02:55.220 --> 00:03:01.320
the misattribution in question, speaks to
how screwed up credit and sourcing and copyright

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.620
are on the internet.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:07.780
We do an especially bad job of acknowledging
one another's work and checking our sources.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:11.750
So Hank, there's my story of how I came to
believe I'd written something I actually hadn't.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:15.880
Hopefully, we've gotten it right now, but
I'm sure that somewhere, someone's copyright

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:19.240
is still being violated in all of this, which
reminds me to tell you, Hank, that starting

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.900
soon over at Crash Course there will be an
introduction to intellectual property and

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.210
copyright law, which is a great interest to
all of us who care about the internet and

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:31.660
also like, art and the making of things, so
you can check that out soon at Crash Course,

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:34.140
and hopefully, together, we can get better
at this stuff.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:38.910
In the meantime, please support Risa and Nerdfighter,
Melody by getting their posters.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.400
Hank, I will see you on Friday.

